WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.740
- On this episode the legend,

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:03.730
and I don't say that lightly,

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.510
the legend Michael Ovitz
is in the building.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:06.782
- This guy cracks me up.

00:00:06.782 --> 00:00:09.449
(hip hop music)

00:00:19.347 --> 00:00:21.100
- Hey everybody, This is Gary Vaynerchuk

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.250
and this is episode 293
of The #AskGaryVee Show

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.122
and this is gonna be one that I will watch

00:00:27.122 --> 00:00:29.780
probably for the rest of
my life every two, four,

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.260
five, seven years, 'cause I'm
gonna think of it very fondly.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.724
I don't throw this around very easily,

00:00:34.724 --> 00:00:37.400
but an absolute legend has walked in.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.280
The title, this book cover
I'm sure you're seeing it,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.840
for people listening on
the podcast Google it,

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.640
Who Is Michael Ovitz is something

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.363
that we're gonna answer here.

00:00:46.363 --> 00:00:48.650
Please Facebook, I know
you're watching right now,

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:49.790
please put in your phone numbers.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:50.870
This is a rare opportunity.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:55.590
Michael is such an icon
and mogul of the culture,

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:56.630
which is what I appreciate

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:57.670
and I'll go into that in a minute,

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.253
but at this point of his career

00:01:00.253 --> 00:01:02.790
and all the things he's focused on,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.100
the ability to actually ask him a question

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.406
is far and few between so
I'm excited that our format

00:01:07.406 --> 00:01:10.440
is gonna allow two or three
of you to ask him a question.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.090
And if you know who he is

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.546
I'm sure you've got seven
or eight questions lined up,

00:01:14.546 --> 00:01:16.610
but for some that don't,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.344
as we start putting down
the blocks in this episode

00:01:19.344 --> 00:01:21.562
I think you're gonna get really excited.

00:01:21.562 --> 00:01:23.540
Michael thank you so much for being here.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.905
- Thanks for having me.

00:01:24.905 --> 00:01:27.314
- For the people that
are watching right now

00:01:27.314 --> 00:01:29.200
that don't know who you are,

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.860
what is the kind of two to
three minute cliff notes of

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.340
who you are professionally, personally,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.386
wherever you want to take it.

00:01:37.386 --> 00:01:39.680
- Someone who started out in the mail room

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.470
at a large agency and went
off with some partners

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.560
to start our own company

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.240
and started what I think to
this day 40-plus years later

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.375
is the definitive artists
agency in the world.

00:01:51.375 --> 00:01:52.550
- And that is?

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.005
- Creative Artists Agency.

00:01:54.005 --> 00:01:56.580
- So for everybody who's
not from the Hollywood world

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.630
or the business world and
you're playing in your own silo,

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.870
CAA is a global leader today.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.292
Michael and his partners
left William Morris,

00:02:03.292 --> 00:02:07.133
which is also a major player
now with a merger, WME.

00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:09.866
In Hollywood land this is royalty.

00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:12.720
In every industry I came more
from the liquor business first

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.410
and then Silicon Valley culture,

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.280
which you've put a big flag in

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.260
and we'll get into that later.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.490
But if you're aware of
the LA business scene,

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:25.070
there are no bigger businesses
that have been built

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.480
in the talent representation agency world,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.540
CAA is at the top.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:33.130
And Michael is really...

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:37.570
I have so many friends who are agents

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.780
in LA and New York and
other parts of the world

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.690
and because of my wine show on YouTube

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.650
started having interest in that world,

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.095
the amount of times I heard...

00:02:46.095 --> 00:02:47.570
in the wine world not as much,

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.407
though Michael has a
nice collection of wine.

00:02:50.407 --> 00:02:54.280
But as soon as I even
sniffed into anything

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.820
that looked like representation
of talent in the world,

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.725
the name Michael Ovitz got
thrown around so many times

00:02:59.725 --> 00:03:03.250
I was super intrigued and so
many people that I admire,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:07.970
my contemporaries, really
unbelievably look up to you.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.474
You really...

00:03:09.474 --> 00:03:12.540
And I don't know how
you'll answer to this,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.170
but like you absolutely
changed the paradigm of

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:20.170
what a talent agent is and was
and to this day looked upon.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.435
I mean I'm not sure if it's
really been replicated.

00:03:23.435 --> 00:03:25.124
Is that something that you think is great,

00:03:25.124 --> 00:03:26.490
is that something that you think is

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:27.700
fabricated by the market,

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.060
is that over a, you know,

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.520
obviously somebody who's an agent

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:31.980
always wants to find that balance

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.380
between the talent being at the forefront,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.010
but you became very well-known and a mogul

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:38.861
and did incredible things.

00:03:38.861 --> 00:03:43.410
How do you reconcile being in
a place in your life right now

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.320
where you can write a book
called Who Is Michael Ovitz

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:46.153
and have it on the cover

00:03:46.153 --> 00:03:48.007
and just have so many
people know who you are

00:03:48.007 --> 00:03:50.200
and the impact you've
had to such a big part.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.390
I mean I think Hollywood,
Michael real quick,

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:53.480
and I'll stop talking soon,

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.940
I think Hollywood is the
great machine of America.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.830
I think America's brand in the world

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:03.830
is actually driven by the
machine that Hollywood was

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.180
in a lot of ways Silicon
Valley and social media is now

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.250
playing a little bit of that role,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.570
but it's kind of unbelievable

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:12.960
that you were top dog of the machine

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.210
that probably allows America
to be top dog in the world.

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:18.692
Do you ever take a step
back and say holy crap?

00:04:18.692 --> 00:04:21.660
- You know there's no
time to take a step back

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.793
and think about that.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.260
When I decided to write
the book it was more about

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.671
trying to have some
record of all the stories

00:04:29.671 --> 00:04:32.270
and all the different things that we did.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.140
But to get right to the
heart of your question

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.784
about what we did, when we
started in the agency business,

00:04:37.784 --> 00:04:41.940
agents basically would
field offers for clients.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:42.944
They would look--

00:04:42.944 --> 00:04:44.020
- They would pick up the phone.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.040
- They'd pick up the phone
and someone would call

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.450
and say is Gary available
for such-and-such a day

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.680
to do such-and-such a project

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.420
and it was pretty much a business

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.580
that moved to a pretty slow clock.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.620
And we made a decision while
we were at William Morris

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.940
and watched this company,
which was terrific,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.390
a great company, but
suffering from a disease

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:07.410
that all companies do,

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.790
which is lack of transition
to younger people

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.280
with younger and fresher ideas.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.080
And the more senior people
were running the company

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.991
and it was basically stalling out

00:05:18.991 --> 00:05:21.760
and we came up with this idea

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.199
that it was really important

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:27.090
for the talent to be in the driver's seat.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:28.290
How do you do that?

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:31.840
How do you get the talent from
being in a second position

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:33.390
to being a first position?

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:36.120
And it was the financers,
the buyers, the studios,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.110
the networks, the publishing
houses, the record companies,

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:41.870
all of whom were in the driver's seat.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:43.590
And we decided what we can do is

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:46.780
we would start to encourage our clients

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.769
to meet with each other, meet
with other creative people,

00:05:49.769 --> 00:05:54.760
and pretty much keep all
their ideas to themselves

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.640
and their group until
we could take them out

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.300
completely as a package.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.038
And by doing that over a period of years

00:06:02.038 --> 00:06:04.860
and within a very short period of time--

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:06.400
- What period of time, just for all of us.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.355
- So we started in December of
'74, officially January '75.

00:06:11.355 --> 00:06:14.970
And it took us probably...

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.180
- Is it true, like I've
done enough homework

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:19.690
and there's been other
books and Google articles

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:21.010
and documentaries,

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.860
you guys were planning to
start a little bit later,

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.590
but then word within
William Morris was heard

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.910
and that's why you guys
had to move quicker?

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:28.930
- Yeah well what happened was,

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:31.100
I went to the City National Bank,

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.220
which was a local bank in Beverly Hills

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.450
for a $100,000 loan for us to get started

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.380
and I didn't think that

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:42.050
they would call the treasurer
of William Morris in New York

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:46.440
and he called the CFO in LA
and he called the president,

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.254
they called us and said
you guys can leave,

00:06:49.254 --> 00:06:54.254
which moved our plan along a
little faster than we expected.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:58.240
- So December, January
'74, '75 and then by when?

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.400
- By early eighties we
were in the position

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.030
where we were selling packaged material

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:05.480
with all of our clients.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:06.710
- And for everybody you know...

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:08.990
I have a lot of people
who probably don't know

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:10.750
all the Hollywood terminology,

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:14.662
package would be like a big
star with a big director

00:07:14.662 --> 00:07:17.693
like what what are the pieces of a package

00:07:17.693 --> 00:07:18.900
in the way that you guys created it?

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.350
- To us a package was
anything that was built around

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:22.860
what we called a motor.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.580
So you needed one client who was a motor

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.500
and let's just take an example--

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:29.940
- Please, use an example,
you have great stories.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.500
So give me a real package.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:33.310
- Well a real package would have been a...

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:34.300
- You're excited now.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:35.760
- A real package would have been,

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.190
let's just take a simple
one, Ghostbusters.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:42.010
So Dan Aykroyd is not just
a brilliant performer,

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:45.080
he's a writer and he
came up with this idea

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:48.200
about three Saturday Night Live type guys

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.930
running around New York
City chasing ghosts.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:55.521
And the one line, what you
just did, you have to laugh.

00:07:55.521 --> 00:08:00.521
The one line gave the
project simultaneously

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.530
to Bill Murray and to Ivan Reitman.

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:06.440
Ivan who was great producer/director.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.321
Ivan had done Stripes with
Bill Murray and Meatballs.

00:08:10.321 --> 00:08:12.230
He produced Animal House,

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:15.580
which was one of the
great comedies of all time

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:20.020
and Bill, nobody funnier than Bill Murray.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:23.687
I mean that sense of humor
is beyond of all time.

00:08:23.687 --> 00:08:25.970
I mean beyond extraordinary.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.210
So Bill and Danny and Ivan met

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:32.300
and they decided on Harold
Ramis, may he rest in peace,

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.440
it was from Chicago's Second City,

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.244
as a co-writer and the third Ghostbuster

00:08:37.244 --> 00:08:41.450
and that put together a
complete film right there.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:45.610
We had three stars, we had three writers,

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:47.070
and we had a director.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.540
- And then you go to
studios and have them,

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:52.960
you reversed the leverage to the package,

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:56.150
which made bids instead of
going and giving the IP---

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:57.030
- Exactly right.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.278
Normally this studio
would come to us and say

00:08:59.278 --> 00:09:01.270
we have this script called Ghostbusters.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:02.783
- And we want your star.

00:09:03.648 --> 00:09:05.783
- Well they'd say we want this director,

00:09:05.783 --> 00:09:09.840
we want this writer, and
they'd come one step at a time.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:11.180
We came with the whole thing

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:13.050
completely put together for them.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:15.450
All they had to do is say yes or no

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:17.800
and we did not give them
much time to say no.

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:19.760
- How quickly did the rest of the market,

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:20.960
so this packaging,

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.410
which gave you guys a
competitive advantage

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:25.627
of a new formula to go to market,

00:09:25.627 --> 00:09:29.620
did the other agencies
quickly jump on board

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:30.730
and start doing it themselves

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:32.870
or do they kind of sit and
push against it for awhile

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.030
and then later succumb
to it and do it as well?

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:39.480
- Well fortunately for us the
competition was slow to react

00:09:40.754 --> 00:09:44.637
and in their inability to move quickly--

00:09:44.637 --> 00:09:47.046
- Audacity. (laughs)

00:09:47.046 --> 00:09:49.540
By the way, it's what's happening
to VaynerMedia right now

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.539
in the moments of their audacity
while they scrutinize it,

00:09:52.539 --> 00:09:53.720
we land grab.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:55.840
- All I know is in the two
weeks since I've been up here,

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:57.555
it looks like you have double the staff.

00:09:57.555 --> 00:09:58.960
(laughing)

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.450
- And so in their slowness...

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.790
- In their slowness we
started to sign other clients

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:07.270
and once we had critical
mass of a majority of that,

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:10.661
it was over because you can't
package without the elements

00:10:10.661 --> 00:10:13.810
and we had three-quarters of the elements

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:15.460
so there was no competition.

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:17.380
And it was the same
problem for the studios.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:21.074
They had absolutely the
ability to make contracts,

00:10:21.074 --> 00:10:24.030
but we had the creative people.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.564
So what we were trying to do
was keep the creative people

00:10:26.564 --> 00:10:29.377
working together and hold their ideas

00:10:29.377 --> 00:10:32.410
until we could put them
together completely.

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:33.347
- Facebook if you're watching live now,

00:10:33.347 --> 00:10:35.160
I know a lot of you
listening on the podcast

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.470
or watching on film, but
if you're watching now

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:37.430
please put in your phone number.

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:39.870
It's an amazing opportunity
to get a question

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:43.200
into one of the legends
of culture in my opinion

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.770
obviously because as we
keep going through this

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:47.600
what Michael was able to do and disrupt,

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.447
not only in Hollywood...

00:10:50.447 --> 00:10:52.160
I'm doing a little of the talking,

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:54.600
bled in by doing some
incredible work for Coca-Cola

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:56.770
and other things and started
to disrupt Madison Avenue

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:58.220
and now in these days,

00:10:58.220 --> 00:10:59.410
and we've spent less
time talking about this

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:00.243
so I don't know as much,

00:11:00.243 --> 00:11:03.180
but spending enormous
amount of times in SF

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:04.630
with the contemporaries in Silicon Valley.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:06.302
So in my opinion

00:11:06.302 --> 00:11:08.700
because I have context
to both the audience

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.570
and Michael's career and what he's up to,

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:15.700
if you were a creator and
you want to ask a question

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:18.550
at a macro and and often if
you're listening or watching

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:21.480
you know how much I talk about
history tells you the future.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.110
I think there's no better time to call now

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:23.943
so put in your phone numbers.

00:11:23.943 --> 00:11:25.270
Give Andy kind of your questions.

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:27.020
Andy's looking right now.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:28.180
So what happens next Michael?

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.390
So mid '70s you started
this company with two other,

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:32.223
more than that, right?

00:11:32.223 --> 00:11:33.593
- There were five of us.

00:11:35.249 --> 00:11:38.330
- Across the way it became kind
of more core like core three

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.219
right, for different reasons.

00:11:40.219 --> 00:11:41.865
- One of the guys retired,

00:11:41.865 --> 00:11:46.210
one of the guys kind of took
a little bit of a backseat,

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:47.830
but he stayed with us,

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:51.160
but it was really Ron Meyer
and Bill Hamer and myself.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:53.000
- You guys had a core
run for quite awhile.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.060
- We had a good run.

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.330
We had a good run.

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:56.800
- It's really incredible, isn't it?

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.780
What was the biggest challenge

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:00.530
of running a three-headed monster?

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:01.363
That's tough.

00:12:01.363 --> 00:12:03.318
I always say two cooks in the
kitchen is already difficult.

00:12:03.318 --> 00:12:05.020
- Well I was really lucky.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:09.160
I had really good partners
and we all split things up

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:11.020
in a really interesting way.

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:15.160
So Ron was fantastic with
the people inside the company

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:19.590
and also was great with
handling actors and actresses

00:12:20.542 --> 00:12:22.240
and Bill, no one better

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:23.890
at putting television shows together.

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:26.485
I mean the guy had this
extraordinary ability

00:12:26.485 --> 00:12:28.716
to read material and ideas

00:12:28.716 --> 00:12:32.210
and just instantaneously figure out

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.067
if he could sell them as TV shows.

00:12:34.067 --> 00:12:39.067
And that left me an extraordinarily
wide open field to run

00:12:39.388 --> 00:12:44.388
so no one really stopped
me from doing anything

00:12:44.561 --> 00:12:49.561
and we had a different idea
every week of what to do,

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.330
a different idea and
these guys supported it.

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:53.950
As a matter of fact when we came up

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.100
with the idea of working for Coca-Cola

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:58.620
and handling all their advertising,

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:01.563
worldwide ad budget of
over 600 million dollars

00:13:01.563 --> 00:13:05.380
and their prior agency
was McCann Erickson.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:08.370
They had 365 account executives.

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:11.442
We came up with this idea
that we could do this

00:13:11.442 --> 00:13:15.782
and do it better and we
could do it for less money

00:13:15.782 --> 00:13:18.600
and we could do it with six people.

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.338
And Bill Haver came in as
one of those six people

00:13:21.338 --> 00:13:26.338
and we produced over 35
commercials a year for 10 years,

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.740
including the polar bears,
which still run to this day.

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:33.530
And we did it with something
that is known well today,

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:35.410
but in those days didn't exist,

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:37.140
it's called outsourcing.

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:38.643
- [Gary] Go figure.

00:13:38.643 --> 00:13:39.730
- What can I tell you.

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:41.630
We were outsourcing animation,

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:43.860
we didn't have to own all the animators,

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.993
we're outsourcing all of the production,

00:13:45.993 --> 00:13:50.480
but our clients basically
directed these spots

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:54.400
and we were doing 35 spots
for the cost of seven

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:57.310
that were being done by the large agency.

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:59.800
And it was incredibly well coordinated

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.800
and Bill spent an enormous
amount of time on it.

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:04.230
- Michael, let's jump to
the book for a second.

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:05.430
When is the book out?

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:06.470
- Tomorrow.

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:07.318
- Amazing.

00:14:07.318 --> 00:14:09.030
- It's out here today,
Gary right in front of you.

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:11.580
- I'm about to sell this, I'm
about to flip this real quick

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:14.035
on the Internet for a margin
because people have to wait.

00:14:14.035 --> 00:14:15.110
- You can sell those with your--

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:16.330
- Exactly.

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:20.570
Michael, what's the one
story in here that is either

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:23.220
a clarification, I mean guys,

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:24.310
I'm telling you when you Google,

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:25.460
whether it's Steven Seagal,

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:28.790
it's incredible the breath.

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:31.350
You know what's amazing
about what Michael has done?

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.000
A lot of us have careers,

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:34.660
but we're known within our industry.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:36.410
What's amazing about being a pioneer

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:39.980
and at the tippy-top and I'm
talking number one of Hollywood

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.720
is like Ghostbusters everyone's like oh!

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:45.960
Or like my favorite stories
of the old days is like

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:49.160
which actor almost was who?

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:52.430
I think you're Ron
managed Sylvester Stallone

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:53.372
is that correct?

00:14:53.372 --> 00:14:54.205
- [Michael] That's correct.

00:14:54.205 --> 00:14:56.110
- So I remember somewhere I heard

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:59.721
that Sylvester Stallone was
almost Beverly Hills Cop.

00:14:59.721 --> 00:15:01.355
Do you know about this?

00:15:01.355 --> 00:15:02.730
- I do. (laughs)

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.157
- I mean I don't know what,

00:15:04.157 --> 00:15:06.078
listen there's a lot of
things that go on here

00:15:06.078 --> 00:15:06.911
that I don't know.

00:15:06.911 --> 00:15:09.690
Like I'm asking you, like
there's truth to that, right?

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:11.110
- There's so many movies.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:13.060
- But tell me that one
'cause that one to me,

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:15.150
when you think Eddie as Beverly Hill Cops

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:18.090
and now you're telling me wait,
Sly was strategically or Ron

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:19.370
or you or whoever was saying

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:20.590
this would be good for your career

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:22.610
because you're so Rambo and Rocky-ed out,

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:24.240
this can show a different side of you.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:26.500
Just the thought that it was this close

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:28.590
to Sylvester Stallone
being Beverly Hills Cop

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:29.423
I think is fascinating.

00:15:29.423 --> 00:15:32.510
- It is true, but you have
to realize that these things,

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:36.440
there are so many films
that had different elements

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:38.640
attached to them and the elements change.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:39.800
- I get it.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:41.837
Give me another one Michael
because I could sit for,

00:15:41.837 --> 00:15:44.175
because to me that's what everybody...

00:15:44.175 --> 00:15:45.950
people like literally in the comments now,

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:47.670
holy shit Sly was almost...

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:50.070
Like what's another one in the 11th hour

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:52.770
that the actor changed
out or somebody said no

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:54.030
and they regretted it forever.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:54.910
- [Michael] There's one on the book.

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:55.743
- Tell me.

00:15:55.743 --> 00:15:57.928
- Schindler's List and Cape Fear

00:15:57.928 --> 00:16:01.833
where Spielberg and Scorsese traded films.

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:07.460
Marty developed Schindler's List

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:10.128
and Steven developed Cape Fear.

00:16:10.128 --> 00:16:11.120
- That's wild.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:13.610
- Steven wanted to do Schindler's List

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:16.110
for a whole series of
reasons, mostly personal.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:18.620
Marty who is one of the...

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:20.930
two of the greatest filmmakers

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:24.890
in the history of the
universe both doing projects

00:16:26.756 --> 00:16:30.550
that they basically
decided would be better

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:32.410
if they switch projects.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:33.343
- That's nuts.

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:36.520
- Yeah like just looking
at all your faces.

00:16:38.168 --> 00:16:41.500
So what's story in here because
there's so many stories,

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.636
what did you either clarify

00:16:43.636 --> 00:16:47.340
and/or what's a story in here
that nobody's heard before?

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.290
- Oh, I couldn't even answer that Gary.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:54.130
There are so many stories
in this book that happened.

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:57.433
The book basically was designed originally

00:16:57.433 --> 00:16:59.970
as 10 deals was the name of the book

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:01.230
and that was 10 years ago.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.662
- And you felt that you
could talk, one more time,

00:17:03.662 --> 00:17:04.495
and that was 10 years ago?

00:17:04.495 --> 00:17:05.328
- 10 years ago.

00:17:05.328 --> 00:17:07.680
- So you thought through a
series of telling stories

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.970
about 10 specific deals
that you could help

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:11.805
all the entrepreneurs and executives

00:17:11.805 --> 00:17:14.770
take the nuances from
these 10 separate deals,

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:16.040
here's a complete package

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:17.470
and a blueprint for you to navigate.

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:19.760
- And what happened while
I was doing it is that

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:22.610
the publisher kept asking
for more information

00:17:22.610 --> 00:17:24.431
and as I was working up in the valley,

00:17:24.431 --> 00:17:27.360
the brilliant young
people that I work with,

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:30.086
the men and women, they
were asking questions about

00:17:30.086 --> 00:17:32.760
why did this work why did this not work?

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.400
They were as interested in the mistakes

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:36.350
as they were in the successes.

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:39.510
So I started to write
more and more and more

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:41.500
and the stories went beyond the deals.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:44.700
They went into actual stories
of the creative process.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:46.180
You know it's really fascinating

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:48.207
and you of all people
will appreciate this,

00:17:48.207 --> 00:17:50.610
how do you define creativity?

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:52.460
I will never forget and it's in the book,

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:55.330
sitting in Japan, I had flown in,

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:57.600
I've been on a 13 hour flight,

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:59.570
went to the hotel, took
a shower, got ready,

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:02.290
went to dinner which is the
middle of the night in LA,

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:05.620
went to dinner in Japan with
the head of Matsushita Electric

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:07.600
and five of his associates.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:09.810
I was with four of my associates from CAA

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:12.352
and in the course of me literally

00:18:12.352 --> 00:18:15.613
having the most extraordinary
time staying awake,

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:18.380
I get asked this question

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:20.620
by this senior Matsushita official:

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:23.350
what is creativity?

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:26.310
Now that question is almost
impossible to answer,

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:28.350
but in a strange way I tried to answer it

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:31.380
or show some peeks of it in the book.

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:34.470
How does a film like Rain Man
or Tootsie or Ghostbusters

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.790
or Jurassic Park or Good Fellows.

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:41.270
How do these movies get put
together from a one-line idea.

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:45.770
So it's one line that's verbal
and you look down the road

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:49.033
and three years later you're
watching it in a movie theater.

00:18:49.033 --> 00:18:53.652
I will never, till the day
I disappear off the planet,

00:18:53.652 --> 00:18:57.720
ever lose sight of what
an extraordinary privilege

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:01.100
it has been to be
involved in that process,

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:03.435
learn the process, be involved with people

00:19:03.435 --> 00:19:07.593
that understand the process
and can actually transform

00:19:07.593 --> 00:19:11.620
an idea into something you
can read, see, or hear.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:14.260
- In hindsight Michael, that's amazing,

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:17.471
in hindsight I really
wanted to ask this question

00:19:17.471 --> 00:19:21.130
and you're gonna play
it off and be humble,

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:24.102
but I really want you to answer
it so please do this for me.

00:19:24.102 --> 00:19:26.360
Now that you can look back on it

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:30.200
and I think about people
I hate like Michael Jordan

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:32.720
and Tom Brady, but this is more business.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:37.229
Like there's this is the life
of a 42 year old New York fan,

00:19:37.229 --> 00:19:40.780
we just happen to luckily
be in the prime eras

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:43.433
of like these players
that are are competitors.

00:19:46.425 --> 00:19:49.320
In hindsight what did
it feel like to truly

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:52.423
be at the top of the game
of a game of that size?

00:19:53.372 --> 00:19:55.900
I'm sure everybody could
define it differently.

00:19:55.900 --> 00:19:58.030
Some may say was an
11-year run, a 3-year run,

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:02.600
a four-day run, I'm not
gonna allow you to dodge it

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:05.097
and say Gary, I never
looked at it that way.

00:20:05.097 --> 00:20:08.330
It's inconceivable that you
didn't understand at some point,

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:11.123
holy cow or I'm so glad I did it,

00:20:11.123 --> 00:20:12.450
this is what I set out to do,

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:15.563
that you had an era where
you were disproportionately

00:20:15.563 --> 00:20:18.097
the most powerful guy in Hollywood.

00:20:18.097 --> 00:20:20.270
How does it, do you feel,

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:23.287
now that you look back on
it did you not realize it?

00:20:23.287 --> 00:20:24.520
Did it feel like it?

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:26.550
Was an over pressure like
what does it feel like

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:30.035
to be in the eye of the storm
in the white heat moment

00:20:30.035 --> 00:20:33.592
for a prolonged period of time
of a game that's so visual?

00:20:33.592 --> 00:20:37.010
- So first of all, you do
feel it and you understand it,

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:39.440
the question is do you believe it?

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:40.640
And I never believed it.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:44.193
I understood it and I'll
tell you why I understood it

00:20:44.193 --> 00:20:46.800
because it's really a good question.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.536
I understood it because we had the ability

00:20:49.536 --> 00:20:52.560
to get things done that
no one else could do.

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:54.973
So when you have that kind of Midas touch

00:20:54.973 --> 00:20:58.465
and you're on what I call a positive role,

00:20:58.465 --> 00:21:02.425
which is that everything that
you're touching is working,

00:21:02.425 --> 00:21:05.604
the amount of films and TV
shows that we put together,

00:21:05.604 --> 00:21:08.665
we had a 15 year run that was unheard of

00:21:08.665 --> 00:21:10.920
and I don't think I'll ever be duplicated.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:11.753
- [Gary] I think that's right.

00:21:11.753 --> 00:21:14.310
- We packaged close to 300 movies.

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:16.740
Probably an equal number
of television shows

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:19.481
and you've seen the list of
the films, they're iconic.

00:21:19.481 --> 00:21:24.210
Now does that mean that you
let all that go to your head?

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:28.270
We tried to basically
handle it as best we could,

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:31.104
but there are others
around you that make it

00:21:31.104 --> 00:21:34.690
that are affected and there
are people that write about you

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:36.750
or people that are jealous

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:39.072
or people that have
been burned in the week

00:21:39.072 --> 00:21:40.710
of the power curve.

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:44.900
There's frankly a lot of negatives

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:47.480
that get rolled into
that great big positive.

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:49.652
So for me I used to try
to say to the staff,

00:21:49.652 --> 00:21:54.652
we have this giant club,
don't ever, ever touch it.

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:57.890
Don't even reach for it.

00:21:57.890 --> 00:22:02.050
Everybody knows that we have this power

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:03.730
to be able to get things made.

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:07.511
When we got Marty Scorsese's
Last Temptation of Christ made,

00:22:07.511 --> 00:22:10.703
which no one could touch or get done.

00:22:12.072 --> 00:22:14.420
And when we got Rain Man made,

00:22:14.420 --> 00:22:18.130
it was a movie that languished
it was put into turn around

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:21.160
by Warner Brothers, which means
they basically rejected it

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:23.570
and said anyone that
wants it could have it.

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:25.720
We went through four directors

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:28.693
and ended up getting it made
and it won six Academy Awards.

00:22:28.693 --> 00:22:33.221
These kinds of things prove
that you have the ability,

00:22:33.221 --> 00:22:37.220
the issue is there are
people in the wake of it.

00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:38.400
- Sure, the collateral.

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:42.286
- It's collateral damage and I must say

00:22:42.286 --> 00:22:47.286
the collateral damage was
bigger than I ever estimated.

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:49.690
- And the collateral is multifaceted

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:51.460
'cause it's not just
professional, it's personal.

00:22:51.460 --> 00:22:52.440
- No, it's everything.

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:54.650
- That's the thing I think
people don't realize, right?

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:56.470
It's not only your employees
or your competitors,

00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:58.708
it is your spouses and children.

00:22:58.708 --> 00:23:01.860
There's there's so much collateral

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:03.080
with that kind of white heat.

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:05.430
- It's a sea of collateral damage

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:07.610
and there's not much you can do about it.

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:09.800
I don't know anyone that's experienced

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:11.490
the kind of run that we did

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:13.460
that didn't have the collateral damage.

00:23:13.460 --> 00:23:15.800
And one of the reasons I wrote the book

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:17.920
is that I came with the realization

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:18.760
that it wasn't so good

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:20.570
that we had all this collateral damage

00:23:20.570 --> 00:23:23.760
and I often ask myself could
we have done it differently?

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:25.871
- And what do you come up with
in your own head nowadays?

00:23:25.871 --> 00:23:30.090
- You know this is gonna
be not a popular answer,

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:34.180
but could have done it a little
differently but not a lot.

00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:36.432
- Yeah because I think. you
know I apologize for jumping in.

00:23:36.432 --> 00:23:40.390
I'm gonna understand that
because as a creative businessman

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:42.943
like look, the reality is I am who I am.

00:23:44.529 --> 00:23:47.960
I'm aware that I'm gonna be
in the similar realization,

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:49.060
I already am about things
that have already happened.

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:52.630
- But what you don't
realize is that you're,

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:57.530
it's like throwing a
stone into a still water

00:23:57.530 --> 00:24:00.000
and you have a series
of concentric circles.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.380
You don't realize that you're the stone.

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:05.110
So it's not the first concentric
circle that's an issue

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:06.600
or the second or the third,

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:09.560
it's the one way out there
that you don't know exists

00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:11.300
and there are people that work for you

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:13.740
and they're all out here,
they're speaking for you.

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:14.580
- [Gary] 100%.

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:16.780
- They don't all say the right thing.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.590
- I default the other way.

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:19.904
I'm really, I mean it.

00:24:19.904 --> 00:24:23.030
The way I tried to thoughtfully
think about it over time

00:24:23.030 --> 00:24:25.905
is I default into when they
are saying the right thing,

00:24:25.905 --> 00:24:28.120
you just can't control certain things.

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:30.270
- So we tried hard to control it

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:32.190
and that's why we were
viewed as controlling

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:36.202
and we were viewed as
having Stepford agents.

00:24:36.202 --> 00:24:37.340
- [Nikki] Hello?

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:39.350
- Hello, Niki its Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:41.754
You're on The #AskGaryVee
Show with Michael Ovitz.

00:24:41.754 --> 00:24:43.850
- [Nikki] Wow, hello.

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:44.683
- Hi.

00:24:44.683 --> 00:24:45.520
- [Nikki] It's an honor.

00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:46.846
- What's your question?

00:24:46.846 --> 00:24:47.679
Thank you.

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:53.610
- [Nikki] So I guess I
should just ask my question.

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:54.905
- Yeah, that's a good idea.

00:24:54.905 --> 00:24:56.900
- [Nikki] I assume. (laughs)

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:58.860
Basically I just wanted to ask

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:01.462
with being with Creative Artists Agency,

00:25:01.462 --> 00:25:04.920
what's something that when you work with

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:08.136
a lot of other people kind of high-profile

00:25:08.136 --> 00:25:10.870
that you don't want them to do,

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:12.930
that you're like oh this
is a mistake they made,

00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:17.060
they could have had been my client but...

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:23.091
- You mean something that
you don't want them to do?

00:25:23.091 --> 00:25:24.830
- [Nikki] Like what's a mistake

00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:29.252
that when you have artists
who want to be your client,

00:25:29.252 --> 00:25:33.500
something that they do that prevents you

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:35.190
from saying yes to them?

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:37.554
- Oh, you mean the other way

00:25:37.554 --> 00:25:40.406
when you're at the at the top like a CAA,

00:25:40.406 --> 00:25:44.934
what would a emerging
actor/actress/director/athlete say

00:25:44.934 --> 00:25:47.623
that could actually
turn off CAA from saying

00:25:47.623 --> 00:25:50.120
you know what you're not
the right client for us,

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:51.238
is that the question?

00:25:51.238 --> 00:25:52.071
- [Nikki] Correct.

00:25:52.071 --> 00:25:53.328
- Interesting.

00:25:53.328 --> 00:25:54.961
- I've never...

00:25:54.961 --> 00:25:56.393
- The answers always yes,

00:25:56.393 --> 00:25:57.900
we want as many clients as possible.

00:25:57.900 --> 00:25:59.760
- Normally the answer is yes

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:01.200
so I've never thought about it that way.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:02.033
- You know what's funny?

00:26:02.033 --> 00:26:04.510
It's funny that both the
you and I reacted that way.

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:06.650
A lot of people talk about
be thoughtful about...

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:09.150
you know my team, very senior
people, lots of great people.

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:11.400
We have to be thoughtful
about who we bring in,

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:13.180
they need to be the right kind of client.

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:16.680
I'm like yes, yes, yes,
you know it's funny

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:18.623
that we both reacted that way.

00:26:18.623 --> 00:26:21.280
Have you ever had a meeting
where you were court...

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:23.077
let's go this way because
I'd love to hear it,

00:26:23.077 --> 00:26:24.862
a play on that question.

00:26:24.862 --> 00:26:27.340
Did you ever court somebody
because you're like oh my god,

00:26:27.340 --> 00:26:29.610
that would be a crown
jewel or that be a big get

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:31.910
for the firm and I know
you went whale hunting,

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:34.280
was there ever as you were
whale hunting through the years

00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:36.517
when you actually got closer
to it you said oh shit,

00:26:36.517 --> 00:26:40.192
she or he is such a headache
or they have this vulnerability

00:26:40.192 --> 00:26:44.090
or somebody in our stable has
emerged as a direct competitor

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:45.120
and we don't have the bandwidth.

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:48.330
Was there ever, to her point,
was there ever any of that?

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:50.170
- Yeah, we had one situation,

00:26:50.170 --> 00:26:53.890
which was really difficult
for us, which is we,

00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:56.300
as you've already mentioned,
we handled Sylvester Stallone

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:59.247
and he was very competitive
with Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:26:59.247 --> 00:27:03.920
and Arnold was somebody that
I really wanted to represent

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:07.960
because he was, first of all,
he's the most terrific guy.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:11.359
He's really smart and he really understood

00:27:11.359 --> 00:27:15.370
the paradigm of the action
movie better than anybody.

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:18.156
If you look back at his body
of work, it's pretty amazing.

00:27:18.156 --> 00:27:20.930
And I went after Arnold and wanted him

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:22.897
and it got back to Sly

00:27:22.897 --> 00:27:25.523
and he wasn't particularly happy about it.

00:27:25.523 --> 00:27:27.055
(laughing)

00:27:27.055 --> 00:27:27.888
- And that's that.

00:27:27.888 --> 00:27:28.960
- That was that.

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:30.860
- Is there a reason you're
asking that question?

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:32.300
Are you going through a path or anything

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:34.074
like you're trying to navigate?

00:27:34.074 --> 00:27:35.980
- [Nikki] Well the reason why I asked is

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:39.089
because I feel like as
someone who's in Los Angeles

00:27:39.089 --> 00:27:42.375
and is along with thousands,

00:27:42.375 --> 00:27:44.462
hundreds of thousands of other people,

00:27:44.462 --> 00:27:48.088
people always ask oh, what
should I do to get into the door

00:27:48.088 --> 00:27:50.626
and so I was like is there
something that I shouldn't do

00:27:50.626 --> 00:27:53.160
to what I'm trying to get into the door

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.740
because it seems like
everybody has an answer

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.030
for how to get into the door,

00:27:57.030 --> 00:27:59.251
but not like hey, make
sure you don't do this.

00:27:59.251 --> 00:28:01.964
- You know I think short
of anything illegal,

00:28:01.964 --> 00:28:04.550
I think anything short
of illegal or immoral,

00:28:04.550 --> 00:28:07.040
you should do whatever
you feel you have to do

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:08.446
to move your career ahead.

00:28:08.446 --> 00:28:11.938
We were lucky, we went
after talented people,

00:28:11.938 --> 00:28:14.725
and at the end of the day talent wins out.

00:28:14.725 --> 00:28:19.725
And I think when you're young
you have to look for breaks.

00:28:19.991 --> 00:28:22.150
You really have to look for breaks

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:25.200
and they may be social,
they may be business,

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:27.750
they may be because you've got an idea.

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:30.492
Let's not forget we spoke about Stallone,

00:28:30.492 --> 00:28:34.125
this guy wrote a script called Rocky

00:28:34.125 --> 00:28:37.677
on spec, on his own dime, and--

00:28:37.677 --> 00:28:39.070
- [Gary] Sold his damn dog.

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:43.046
- Everybody wanted to buy that
script and move him out of it

00:28:43.046 --> 00:28:47.350
and to his credit he
said no me, no script.

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:49.753
- That's hard when your hungry.

00:28:49.753 --> 00:28:53.710
- It's really hard and took
a lot of guts to do that

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:57.050
because he was offered a lot
of money for that script.

00:28:57.050 --> 00:28:58.350
- The thing that's different today

00:28:58.350 --> 00:29:00.640
than some of those years past for Sly

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:03.520
or when you were at CAA is people can now

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:04.590
go direct to consumer.

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:06.700
You can now produce content

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:09.225
and use the distribution
of an Instagram or YouTube

00:29:09.225 --> 00:29:11.940
or a podcast to absolutely
give you leverage

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:14.410
to be courted in a very interesting way

00:29:14.410 --> 00:29:15.860
in the way that you created packaging

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:17.560
with leveraging the studios,

00:29:17.560 --> 00:29:19.756
the human can now create content

00:29:19.756 --> 00:29:22.425
to build leverage to
have people bid for them.

00:29:22.425 --> 00:29:23.849
- [Micheal] Absolutely.

00:29:23.849 --> 00:29:24.990
- That's what happened to
me by the way, real quick,

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.510
my little quick CA story
is I started a wine show

00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:30.685
on YouTube in 2006 and July of 2007

00:29:30.685 --> 00:29:34.010
after showing up on Conan on late-night

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:35.600
because the producers reached out to me

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:37.030
because they were watching it on YouTube,

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:40.580
the next morning I had
an email from Endeavor,

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:42.840
before the merger, William Morris, others.

00:29:42.840 --> 00:29:46.120
Everybody ironically but CAA.

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:48.465
Then said on my show that
I was going to Hollywood

00:29:48.465 --> 00:29:51.200
to find an agent, it
was like this funny joke

00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:52.540
because I thought it was crazy

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:55.100
and then I got an email from
some people that worked at CAA,

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:57.520
they were having a fantasy
football draft that day

00:29:57.520 --> 00:30:00.267
and they said why don't you
come and hang out with us.

00:30:00.267 --> 00:30:02.320
I said yes, but they didn't
say they were from CAA,

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:03.230
they said what are you doing here?

00:30:03.230 --> 00:30:05.257
I said I'm going to see
these Hollywood agents,

00:30:05.257 --> 00:30:07.463
they said are you gonna see CAA?

00:30:07.463 --> 00:30:09.020
I said no and they made it happen

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:10.720
and that's who I ended up signing with.

00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:13.480
That was all based on
me producing a wine show

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:15.758
in a store in Springfield, New Jersey.

00:30:15.758 --> 00:30:19.090
There was no moving to
LA and waiting on tables

00:30:19.090 --> 00:30:20.670
and going to auditions.

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:22.980
We have an incredible world we live in now

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:25.290
where you can create the leverage for you

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:26.400
on the back of the Internet.

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:29.350
- Different time, completely different.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:33.439
In our day there were roughly 25 companies

00:30:33.439 --> 00:30:38.090
that controlled the complete gate.

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:40.590
In other words the barrier
to entry was severe.

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:43.086
You had seven studios,
you had four networks,

00:30:43.086 --> 00:30:44.730
you had five publishing houses,

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:46.260
you had five record companies,

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:49.360
and probably 10 companies in Europe

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:50.470
that controlled everything

00:30:50.470 --> 00:30:52.300
that you would read, see, and hear.

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:54.999
Our company had relationships
with all these companies.

00:30:54.999 --> 00:30:58.395
If you were a client, you got in the door.

00:30:58.395 --> 00:31:00.320
If you weren't a client,

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:02.990
you had trouble getting in the door.

00:31:02.990 --> 00:31:05.710
Today, this person that called in

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:08.439
if they could make
something three minutes long

00:31:08.439 --> 00:31:12.077
using their iPhone or a small Sony camera,

00:31:12.077 --> 00:31:14.989
they could put it up and if it went viral,

00:31:14.989 --> 00:31:16.620
good god, who knows?

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:18.050
- Kiki do you love me, the dance,

00:31:18.050 --> 00:31:20.914
he's in Drake's video four months later.

00:31:20.914 --> 00:31:23.104
One video on Instagram, it goes.

00:31:23.104 --> 00:31:24.190
I mean just an incredible time.

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:25.640
- And it's the only song

00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:27.800
my three-year-old grandson wants to hear.

00:31:27.800 --> 00:31:28.710
- I get it.

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:29.543
(phone ringing)

00:31:29.543 --> 00:31:30.376
Who's this?

00:31:34.133 --> 00:31:34.966
Brad?

00:31:36.249 --> 00:31:37.830
Let's see what brad has to say Michael

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:40.520
and then we'll go back a
little bit into the book.

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:42.010
- [Brad] Good afternoon, this is Brad.

00:31:42.010 --> 00:31:43.480
- Brad this is Gary Vaynerchuk

00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:46.083
and you're on The #AskGaryVee
Show with Michael Ovitz.

00:31:46.083 --> 00:31:49.566
- [Brad] Oh my gosh, I'm having
an out-of-body experience.

00:31:49.566 --> 00:31:50.910
(laughing)

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:52.866
- That is a very good thing my friend.

00:31:52.866 --> 00:31:55.960
- [Brad] Thank you so
much for taking my call.

00:31:55.960 --> 00:31:58.860
- No worries, what is
your question my friend?

00:31:58.860 --> 00:32:02.290
- [Brad] Well I know that Mr. Ovitz

00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:06.450
you had a tough slog with
Disney that ended poorly,

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.164
just wanted to get your comments on

00:32:08.164 --> 00:32:12.260
what lessons you took from that session?

00:32:12.260 --> 00:32:14.087
- Don't go to Disney.

00:32:14.087 --> 00:32:16.232
(laughing)

00:32:16.232 --> 00:32:18.780
Now that doesn't include the theme parks,

00:32:18.780 --> 00:32:19.939
I happen to like them.

00:32:19.939 --> 00:32:21.630
No, as I said in the book,

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:23.560
it's the biggest career
mistake I ever made.

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:25.990
I completely misjudged it

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:28.668
and it's a really good example of how

00:32:28.668 --> 00:32:32.454
and why not to be your own counsel.

00:32:32.454 --> 00:32:35.470
- It's funny you brought that
up once when we first met

00:32:35.470 --> 00:32:37.360
about not being your own counsel

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:38.881
and the value of why
we're sitting together.

00:32:38.881 --> 00:32:40.360
For everybody at home just give them

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:41.670
the one to two minute cliff notes.

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:43.220
Obviously the three of us
know what we're talking about,

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:45.140
but I think would be fascinating
for everybody at home.

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:46.710
What is the Disney chapter?

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:48.377
- The Disney chapter is about...

00:32:48.377 --> 00:32:53.377
starts with my set of
meetings with Michael Eisner.

00:32:53.458 --> 00:32:57.198
It goes through the time
when he had a heart attack

00:32:57.198 --> 00:33:01.856
and I stayed in the hospital
with him for five to six days

00:33:01.856 --> 00:33:04.468
and he asked me to come on board

00:33:04.468 --> 00:33:07.046
to originally be the co-CEO

00:33:07.046 --> 00:33:11.030
and that went back down
to being the President/COO

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:13.910
and then it went to be the president

00:33:13.910 --> 00:33:15.950
and then when I showed up at his house

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:18.283
for our first meeting prior to starting,

00:33:18.283 --> 00:33:20.570
all the people who are
supposed to report to me

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:22.770
he decided weren't gonna report to me.

00:33:22.770 --> 00:33:24.851
So it was kind of over before it started.

00:33:24.851 --> 00:33:26.475
- Yeah and did you know
that at that point?

00:33:26.475 --> 00:33:28.290
- Well I knew it the minute.

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:29.123
- That point you knew.

00:33:29.123 --> 00:33:31.620
- I knew it when I was
sitting in his living room

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:34.307
at his house when he had the CFO

00:33:34.307 --> 00:33:36.090
and the chief legal officer there

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:38.530
and they said they
weren't gonna report to me

00:33:38.530 --> 00:33:41.010
and he didn't do anything about it.

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:41.843
- You said okay, this is--

00:33:41.843 --> 00:33:43.539
- Well I mean if he was the CEO

00:33:43.539 --> 00:33:45.850
that really wanted this to work

00:33:45.850 --> 00:33:46.990
and he wanted a partner.

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:50.130
I mean what we were trying to
do was set up a partnership

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:52.464
the way Bob Daley and
Terry Semel had at Warners

00:33:52.464 --> 00:33:55.615
or Don kilo and Roberto
Goizueta at Coca Cola.

00:33:55.615 --> 00:34:00.290
Or Tom Murphy and Dan
Burke at ABC Cap Cities.

00:34:00.290 --> 00:34:02.351
These were powerful partnerships.

00:34:02.351 --> 00:34:04.196
These were big companies.

00:34:04.196 --> 00:34:06.283
Disney was about to buy ABC,

00:34:06.283 --> 00:34:09.130
it was going to be a behemoth company,

00:34:09.130 --> 00:34:11.230
more than one person could run.

00:34:11.230 --> 00:34:14.032
So the concept was to create
a dynamic partnership.

00:34:14.032 --> 00:34:15.140
- Understood.

00:34:15.140 --> 00:34:17.104
Thanks for the call brother.

00:34:17.104 --> 00:34:19.792
- [Brad] Thanks Gary, I
wanted to ask quick question,

00:34:19.792 --> 00:34:22.250
what do you know about Okanagan wine?

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:23.440
- I know that the Pinot Noir

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:25.180
from that region super intrigued me,

00:34:25.180 --> 00:34:28.040
like right before I made the
transition to Silicon Valley

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:31.208
from the wine world I
thought that British Columbia

00:34:31.208 --> 00:34:36.110
and Vancouver and Okanagan
region specifically

00:34:36.110 --> 00:34:39.850
were making wines that I
thought were world-class,

00:34:39.850 --> 00:34:42.010
but nobody knew how to market them.

00:34:42.010 --> 00:34:44.360
In the same way that I look
at Baja California Mexico.

00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:47.090
I think the two best wines in the world

00:34:47.090 --> 00:34:50.813
that nobody knows anything
about are Okanagan Pinot Noirs

00:34:50.813 --> 00:34:53.690
and Baja California Cabernets from Mexico

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:55.793
because if you roll up on
anybody and talk about wine

00:34:55.793 --> 00:34:59.360
Mexico and Vancouver are not
coming out of their mouth.

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:00.760
So what I know about them is

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:03.000
they're obnoxiously high quality

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:05.363
with absolutely no brand awareness.

00:35:06.634 --> 00:35:08.254
- [Brad] Fantastic.

00:35:08.254 --> 00:35:11.640
- And I thought Okanagan was in Japan.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:13.687
- I know you're a big, you know,

00:35:13.687 --> 00:35:15.900
you love Japan, huh?

00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:16.900
- I love Japan.

00:35:16.900 --> 00:35:18.968
- Do you still do you go
there often at this point?

00:35:18.968 --> 00:35:22.103
- I haven't recently, but
I used to go once a month.

00:35:22.103 --> 00:35:23.150
- Once a month?!

00:35:23.150 --> 00:35:24.120
- Once a month, yeah.

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:25.680
- Oh man, that's hardcore.

00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:26.513
We're gonna leave it off here.

00:35:26.513 --> 00:35:28.720
I want to use the final time for Michael,

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:31.340
I have so many entrepreneurs,
young entrepreneurs,

00:35:31.340 --> 00:35:33.350
all ages but I know the base of

00:35:33.350 --> 00:35:34.450
who's listening to this show,

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:37.940
a lot of 15 to 35 with plenty older,

00:35:37.940 --> 00:35:40.220
but like what's at this point

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:42.180
as somebody who spent the last decade plus

00:35:42.180 --> 00:35:44.401
in Silicon Valley culture
and all those leaders,

00:35:44.401 --> 00:35:47.208
obviously LA just a man
of the world with Japan

00:35:47.208 --> 00:35:49.100
and New York and just you've played it.

00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:50.430
You've played it at the highest levels

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:52.150
for the last three, four, five decades.

00:35:52.150 --> 00:35:57.052
Like what do you think
is the universal things

00:35:57.052 --> 00:35:58.830
that people should be thinking about

00:35:58.830 --> 00:36:01.240
regardless of the Internet, not Internet,

00:36:01.240 --> 00:36:03.229
regardless of the time, no networks,

00:36:03.229 --> 00:36:05.450
unlimited networks with the Internet.

00:36:05.450 --> 00:36:08.170
What are the traits or the characteristics

00:36:08.170 --> 00:36:13.170
when what you've had is a
really amazing first eye view

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:16.948
into both creative and business titans

00:36:16.948 --> 00:36:19.200
that have been able to be successful?

00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:20.040
It's not just...

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:21.750
that's what's so beautiful
about the business you had

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:23.690
and much of what I like about Vayner,

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:26.300
it's then I get other
views into the world.

00:36:26.300 --> 00:36:27.806
You've had that at the highest levels.

00:36:27.806 --> 00:36:31.040
Are there a theme or two of individuals

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:32.100
that are able to succeed

00:36:32.100 --> 00:36:33.600
or is there anything that stands out?

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:35.090
- It's a great question.

00:36:35.090 --> 00:36:38.880
Nothing has changed for
me from the day I started

00:36:38.880 --> 00:36:40.680
in the entertainment
business through today

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:42.530
when I work up in the valley

00:36:42.530 --> 00:36:44.580
between San Francisco and San Jose.

00:36:44.580 --> 00:36:46.644
It is all about the person.

00:36:46.644 --> 00:36:49.431
It's all about the person,
nothing else matters to me.

00:36:49.431 --> 00:36:52.104
They could have an idea
that you think is good.

00:36:52.104 --> 00:36:55.387
If the person's great,
invest in the person.

00:36:55.387 --> 00:36:57.370
Maybe their second or third idea

00:36:57.370 --> 00:37:00.430
will be better than their
first, but it's all about--

00:37:00.430 --> 00:37:02.520
- Give me a historic story in pop culture

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:03.480
that I think we'd all enjoy

00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:05.180
that you so believed in the person

00:37:05.180 --> 00:37:07.090
where their first idea wasn't as strong

00:37:07.090 --> 00:37:09.670
and the second one was,
they made, I don't know,

00:37:09.670 --> 00:37:11.660
Transformers, you know whatever.

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:14.750
- Well you can frankly
look to almost any director

00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:16.881
and their early work you know,

00:37:16.881 --> 00:37:19.670
but you look at their early work,

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:22.330
it doesn't look anything
like their mature work.

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:24.413
Or you look at some of the
actors that started out,

00:37:24.413 --> 00:37:27.082
I mean Sydney Pollack
who is a great director

00:37:27.082 --> 00:37:30.980
who was very important to
CAA, started out as an actor

00:37:30.980 --> 00:37:34.553
and frankly he wasn't a great
actor when he started out.

00:37:34.553 --> 00:37:36.070
He started out with Redford

00:37:36.070 --> 00:37:38.900
and Redford turned out
to be a better actor

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:41.180
and Sydney turned out to be
an extraordinary director

00:37:41.180 --> 00:37:44.210
who then became a great actor

00:37:44.210 --> 00:37:46.207
and if you remember his role in Tootsie

00:37:46.207 --> 00:37:47.860
where he played the agent,

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:50.270
which I thought was really kind of funny

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:51.810
since I got to coach him.

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:54.876
And everyone starts out slow

00:37:54.876 --> 00:37:57.486
and the careers that to me are the best

00:37:57.486 --> 00:37:59.979
are like trains coming out of a station.

00:37:59.979 --> 00:38:03.080
I am not big on instantaneous stardom.

00:38:03.080 --> 00:38:04.227
I don't think---

00:38:04.227 --> 00:38:05.940
- You think it's a vulnerability?

00:38:05.940 --> 00:38:07.070
- I think it's a huge vulnerability

00:38:07.070 --> 00:38:09.390
because there's nowhere to go but down.

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:11.090
- Can you think of an example of somebody

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:13.240
who's able to calibrate instant fame

00:38:13.240 --> 00:38:15.610
and then build on top of that over time?

00:38:15.610 --> 00:38:17.167
Does anything come to
mind when I say that?

00:38:17.167 --> 00:38:18.000
- Not really.

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:21.050
I mean I don't want to comment
on anybody's career per se,

00:38:21.050 --> 00:38:23.060
but I will say that in the music business

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:24.865
it's a big danger right now.

00:38:24.865 --> 00:38:26.150
- Yeah one-hit wonder as a first song.

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:27.430
Yeah, it's tough.

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:29.370
And it happens so fast.

00:38:29.370 --> 00:38:31.170
- It happens fast, they come and go,

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:32.832
and it's really unfortunate.

00:38:32.832 --> 00:38:35.330
We were in the business
of building careers Gary,

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:37.050
we were in the business--

00:38:37.050 --> 00:38:37.883
- [Gary] Marathons.

00:38:37.883 --> 00:38:39.776
- Well we were in for the long run.

00:38:39.776 --> 00:38:41.918
When you meet Tom Cruise at 19

00:38:41.918 --> 00:38:44.350
you want to make sure that it lasts,

00:38:44.350 --> 00:38:46.640
especially when you believe in the person.

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:50.487
You don't want to have
somebody flame out quickly

00:38:50.487 --> 00:38:54.600
and by the way with bringing
up the point that you raised,

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:56.430
when you look at today's world

00:38:56.430 --> 00:39:00.744
with instantaneous communication
and 5G around the corner,

00:39:00.744 --> 00:39:03.250
it's just too easy to flame out.

00:39:03.250 --> 00:39:04.950
- You know what I love about you?

00:39:04.950 --> 00:39:07.540
That I have so many
people I interact with,

00:39:07.540 --> 00:39:10.320
the fact that I believe that 5G

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:14.170
is one of the most outrageous
technologies that are here,

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:16.010
this is not like wait to, what have you.

00:39:16.010 --> 00:39:18.047
And the fact that so few
people are bringing it up

00:39:18.047 --> 00:39:21.150
and the fact that you drop
that subtly in this interview

00:39:21.150 --> 00:39:23.160
is like I personally enjoy that.

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:25.960
I tend to do things like that,
make references to things,

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:28.440
like do you believe that anybody,

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:30.390
like are you stunned by how few people

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:32.660
understand how big of a technology change

00:39:32.660 --> 00:39:34.700
5G is gonna be over the next couple years?

00:39:34.700 --> 00:39:39.518
- What I'm stunned by is
how few people in the world

00:39:39.518 --> 00:39:44.020
really have grasped the
changes the technology

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:46.460
are bringing right now
beyond what they think.

00:39:46.460 --> 00:39:49.974
They think they're big,
but they're humongous

00:39:49.974 --> 00:39:52.550
and when they start
looking at what's happening

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:55.630
coming down the road, 5G
is the tip of the iceberg.

00:39:55.630 --> 00:39:58.920
Let's get into AI, it's
a whole different game.

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:03.260
I mean the applications for
this are beyond comprehension,

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:07.170
particularly to businesses
that are stratified businesses.

00:40:07.170 --> 00:40:08.940
These businesses, these big businesses,

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:12.380
the fortune 500 companies,
this has got to be

00:40:12.380 --> 00:40:15.230
at the very top of their
agenda of priorities.

00:40:15.230 --> 00:40:16.440
- Yeah but it's not because everything is

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:17.730
Wall Street dynamics and they're trying to

00:40:17.730 --> 00:40:19.960
maximize margin every 90 days.

00:40:19.960 --> 00:40:22.260
- One of the things I learned early on is

00:40:22.260 --> 00:40:23.871
don't think in quarters

00:40:23.871 --> 00:40:27.954
and I learned that by
traveling to Asia in the '80s.

00:40:27.954 --> 00:40:31.373
Don't think in quarters,
start thinking long term.

00:40:31.373 --> 00:40:36.310
Watching how different cultures
react to different things,

00:40:36.310 --> 00:40:38.570
the other thing I learned
in Asia, which was great,

00:40:38.570 --> 00:40:40.650
is have some respect for your elders

00:40:40.650 --> 00:40:42.900
and now that I'm an elder
I like that respect.

00:40:42.900 --> 00:40:44.889
- You like that much
more now than back then.

00:40:44.889 --> 00:40:45.722
(laughing)

00:40:45.722 --> 00:40:49.380
What parting shots, what did
we not cover in this session

00:40:49.380 --> 00:40:50.480
that you'd like to talk about

00:40:50.480 --> 00:40:52.220
or anything that's on your mind

00:40:52.220 --> 00:40:54.510
or things that you'd like
people to think about?

00:40:54.510 --> 00:40:57.070
- I think you've covered
pretty much everything.

00:40:57.070 --> 00:41:00.700
I mean there's a zillion
stories in The Naked City.

00:41:00.700 --> 00:41:03.167
And I've got a lot of stories in the book.

00:41:03.167 --> 00:41:04.860
- Let me ask one thing, this,

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:07.330
the four to seven to 11 people

00:41:07.330 --> 00:41:09.690
that you gave this book to
already that have read it.

00:41:09.690 --> 00:41:11.230
These are clearly people
that have been your friends

00:41:11.230 --> 00:41:13.580
for 30 years or a family member,

00:41:13.580 --> 00:41:16.060
what's been the feedback
you've gotten from this book

00:41:16.060 --> 00:41:19.480
that either feels best
and/or is most surprising

00:41:19.480 --> 00:41:20.920
that you didn't see, but three people

00:41:20.920 --> 00:41:22.320
have commented on that already?

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:25.500
- Well the person that I gave the book to

00:41:25.500 --> 00:41:27.483
through the whole writing of the book

00:41:27.483 --> 00:41:29.225
is the person I live with.

00:41:29.225 --> 00:41:33.274
Tamara Mellon and she's the
one who would say to me--

00:41:33.274 --> 00:41:35.437
- Your full of shit, change that.

00:41:35.437 --> 00:41:36.996
- This really stinks.

00:41:36.996 --> 00:41:38.540
(laughing)

00:41:38.540 --> 00:41:39.650
You know, this is awful,

00:41:39.650 --> 00:41:42.360
this is the person who I trust in.

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:44.410
She's been pretty positive
on the end result.

00:41:44.410 --> 00:41:46.180
- And how's it feel the
day before the book?

00:41:46.180 --> 00:41:47.013
Are you excited?

00:41:47.013 --> 00:41:48.850
I mean you were you know...

00:41:48.850 --> 00:41:51.170
am I correct like you not...

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:54.080
but you had some, like
you were very interested

00:41:54.080 --> 00:41:55.690
in the publishing game back in the day

00:41:55.690 --> 00:41:57.960
and like you saw a lot of
opportunity in that space

00:41:57.960 --> 00:41:59.705
that other people didn't see.

00:41:59.705 --> 00:42:01.850
- We represented usually

00:42:01.850 --> 00:42:05.480
half the New York Times
bestseller list on a weekly basis.

00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:07.230
- So as somebody who
sat on the other side--

00:42:07.230 --> 00:42:09.090
- This has been the
most bizarre experience.

00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:11.540
Starting with when I
started talking to you.

00:42:11.540 --> 00:42:13.060
I told you that when we talked.

00:42:13.060 --> 00:42:13.960
This is insane.

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:15.850
I planned the publicity trips.

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:19.360
I planned all of the different
shows that people would do.

00:42:19.360 --> 00:42:23.500
I would supervise anything
that the big authors would do.

00:42:23.500 --> 00:42:24.850
And I would sometimes sit in

00:42:24.850 --> 00:42:27.410
when they did some of the interviews.

00:42:27.410 --> 00:42:29.780
I find this to be absolutely hysterical

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:32.374
that I'm on this side of the
table, I can't believe it.

00:42:32.374 --> 00:42:33.880
- If you're watching,

00:42:33.880 --> 00:42:36.160
I highly recommend you
pick this up on Amazon

00:42:36.160 --> 00:42:37.610
or at your local bookstore.

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:38.670
I have a very funny...

00:42:38.670 --> 00:42:41.430
Listen, a lot of you know my spiel.

00:42:41.430 --> 00:42:43.476
I'm unbelievably passionate about

00:42:43.476 --> 00:42:45.365
history telling you the future

00:42:45.365 --> 00:42:47.740
and when you get to see players play it

00:42:47.740 --> 00:42:49.564
at the highest level and articulate,

00:42:49.564 --> 00:42:51.602
especially in storytelling form,

00:42:51.602 --> 00:42:55.370
I know that this audience
especially how it reacts to me

00:42:55.370 --> 00:42:56.510
picks up on those nuances.

00:42:56.510 --> 00:42:58.340
I think this will be extremely beneficial

00:42:58.340 --> 00:43:01.300
to a lot of you whether
you know Michael deeply

00:43:01.300 --> 00:43:02.280
or don't know him at all.

00:43:02.280 --> 00:43:04.090
I have a funny feeling a
lot of you'll appreciate it

00:43:04.090 --> 00:43:05.785
so pick it up, support this man,

00:43:05.785 --> 00:43:08.190
and Micheal I appreciate you being on.

00:43:08.190 --> 00:43:09.023
- Thank you so much Gary.

00:43:09.023 --> 00:43:11.083
- Now, question of the day.

00:43:11.083 --> 00:43:11.916
- Question of the day.

00:43:11.916 --> 00:43:13.680
- This is our thing for every guest,

00:43:13.680 --> 00:43:15.190
you get to ask the question,

00:43:15.190 --> 00:43:17.690
what is your question to the VaynerNation?

00:43:17.690 --> 00:43:20.537
- My question is pretty simple.

00:43:20.537 --> 00:43:25.537
We are in the middle right now
of audience viewership change

00:43:27.227 --> 00:43:30.889
and we're dead center in the middle of it.

00:43:30.889 --> 00:43:34.540
We come from a world, at least I do,

00:43:34.540 --> 00:43:37.660
where we watch things day
and date and now we're in

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:40.397
a world where everything
is streamed on-demand

00:43:40.397 --> 00:43:43.000
and I want to know what
the audience thinks

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:45.286
of this new change and what it's done also

00:43:45.286 --> 00:43:49.475
to the uniqueness of the
film business with television

00:43:49.475 --> 00:43:51.621
and the on-demand world

00:43:51.621 --> 00:43:55.313
has kind of taken the spark
out of the film business.

00:43:55.313 --> 00:43:56.930
- Yeah, I think that's right.

00:43:56.930 --> 00:43:59.558
Michael, thank you so much
my friend, good health.

00:43:59.558 --> 00:44:03.553
You keep asking questions,
we'll keep answering them.

00:44:03.553 --> 00:44:06.303
(hip hop music)

